mmediately before the Landlord. My hand contains strong control cards (A, 2, Black Joker) but also weak cards (5, 5, 6, J) that are difficult to lead with. If I play the Ace now, I beat my teammate and force myself to lead, likely with a weak card that the Landlord can easily exploit. If I pass, I force the Landlord to respond to the Queen. If the Landlord plays a King or Ace, I can then trap them with my 2 or Black Joker. If the Landlord passes, my teammate retains the lead, which is beneficial. Therefore, passing is the strategic choice to support the team and save high cards for the Landlord.\n\nReason: Beating the teammate\'s Queen with an Ace is unnecessary and detrimental as it exposes my weak remaining cards (5, 5, 6, J) to the Landlord upon leading. Passing puts pressure on the Landlord to beat the Queen; if they do, I can use my top cards (2, Black Joker) to shut them down later. If they don\'t, my teammate wins the trick.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
